# Part Zero — Gender Studies from Zero

> Before you can answer a single question, you need the ideas, the words and the story of the subject. This part gives you all three, assuming you start from nothing.

## 0.1  What this subject is — and what it is not

**Gender Studies** is the academic study of how societies turn biological males and females into "men" and "women", what roles and values they attach to each, and how these arrangements create inequality in power, resources and opportunity. It also studies people who do not fit neatly into two categories, and it asks how things could be made fairer.

It grew out of an older field, **Women's Studies**, which was born in American universities around ==1969–1970== as the academic arm of the women's movement. Women's Studies asked: where are the women in history, literature, economics and politics? Gender Studies, which spread from the ==1980s==, widened the question: how do femininity *and* masculinity work, how do they relate to each other, and how do they combine with class, race, religion and sexuality?

::: simple
Women's Studies puts women at the centre of the picture. Gender Studies looks at the whole picture — women, men and everyone else — and at the relationships between them.
:::

The subject is **multidisciplinary**: it borrows tools from sociology, anthropology, psychology, history, economics, political science, law, literature, biology and development studies. That is why the CSS paper can move from Freud (psychology) to structural adjustment (economics) to the Hudood Ordinances (law) to Malala Yousafzai (a living case study) in a single sitting.

::: table | What the paper is — and what it is not.
@widths 1,1
The paper IS … | The paper is NOT …
a test of **concepts** — gender, patriarchy, social construction, feminism, empowerment, intersectionality | a test of opinions about men and women
a test of **theory** — the schools of feminism, the development theories, the theories of violence | a sermon for or against feminism
a test of **application** — health, education, work, law, politics and violence in Pakistan | a Western textbook recited without Pakistan in it
a test of **evidence** — thinkers, data, laws, reports and cases | a collection of emotional statements
a test of **structure** — headings, diagrams, a clear argument and a balanced conclusion | a test of memorising long paragraphs
:::

## 0.2  Sex and gender — the first distinction

Everything in this subject begins with one distinction. It was first made in psychology by **Robert Stoller** (*Sex and Gender*, ==1968==) and brought into sociology by the British sociologist **Ann Oakley** in *Sex, Gender and Society* (==1972==).

::: define Sex | biological difference
The biological and physiological characteristics that distinguish females and males: chromosomes (usually XX or XY), hormones, and reproductive organs.
-> Sex is mostly given by birth and is broadly similar across societies.
:::

::: define Gender | social difference
The roles, behaviours, expectations, identities and power that a society attaches to being female or male.
-> Gender is learnt, it varies between societies, and it changes over time — which means it can be changed by policy, law and education.
:::

::: fig compare | Sex and gender compared: the distinction on which the whole subject rests.
@vs vs
## Sex — biology
- Chromosomes, hormones, organs
- Given at birth
- Broadly the same across the world
- Changes very little over history
- Example: only women can give birth
## Gender — society
- Roles, expectations, identities, power
- Learnt through socialisation
- Different in Karachi, Kabul and Copenhagen
- Changes over generations
- Example: the belief that only women should raise children
:::

::: example | The same body, different genders
In rural Punjab, a girl may be expected to stay at home after puberty, to help with cooking and cattle, and to marry young. In Islamabad, a girl of the same age may attend a co-educational university and plan a career. Their bodies — their *sex* — are the same. What their families and communities expect of them — their *gender* — is very different. That difference is what Gender Studies studies.
:::

The distinction was a breakthrough because it separated what is *fixed* from what is *changeable*. If women earn less because of biology, nothing can be done. If they earn less because of gender — because of social rules about who works outside the home — then those rules can be changed.

Later thinkers went further. **Judith Butler** (*Gender Trouble*, ==1990==) and the **queer theorists** asked whether even "sex" is partly social: who decides that there are exactly two sexes, and what of intersex people born with characteristics of both? This debate — asked in CSS ==2018==, ==2024== and ==2026== — is taught fully in Topic 1.

::: trap | Two debates, not one
The **sex versus gender** debate asks *what* is biological and what is social. The **nature versus nurture** debate asks *why* women and men behave differently: because of genes and hormones, or because of upbringing and culture. The two are linked, but they are not the same question. Name both separately when a question mentions either.
:::

## 0.3  The twelve ideas that unlock the whole subject

If you understand these twelve ideas, you can follow any chapter in these notes. Each is taught fully in its topic; here you meet them once, simply.

1. **Patriarchy** — literally "the rule of the father". A social system in which men hold most of the power in the family, the economy, politics and religion, and in which male authority is treated as natural. Almost every theory in this subject is a theory of patriarchy: where it comes from and how it can end.
2. **Gender roles** — the tasks and behaviours a society assigns to each gender. The man as breadwinner and the woman as homemaker is the classic pair.
3. **Socialisation** — the process by which children learn their gender roles from family, school, religion, media and friends. A boy told "boys don't cry" is being socialised.
4. **Social construction** — the idea that something which looks natural is in fact made by society. To say "gender is socially constructed" is to say it was built by people, and so can be rebuilt.
5. **The sexual division of labour** — the split of work into "men's work" (paid, public, valued) and "women's work" (unpaid, domestic, invisible). Care work in the home is the largest unpaid industry in the world.
6. **Public and private spheres** — the public sphere is politics, markets and paid work; the private sphere is home and family. Patriarchy has kept women in the private sphere. Feminists answered: **"the personal is political"** — what happens at home is shaped by power too.
7. **Masculinity and femininity** — the qualities a society expects of men (strength, control, providing) and of women (care, modesty, obedience). There are many masculinities; the dominant one, which Raewyn Connell called **hegemonic masculinity**, is held up as the ideal and harms women and many men.
8. **Feminism** — the belief that women and men should have equal rights and opportunities, and the movement to achieve it. There are many feminisms (liberal, radical, Marxist, and more), which agree that women are disadvantaged but disagree about why and what to do.
9. **Intersectionality** — a term coined by **Kimberlé Crenshaw** in ==1989==. Gender never works alone: a poor, rural, minority woman faces gender *plus* class *plus* religion *plus* location, and these overlap to produce a unique disadvantage.
10. **Empowerment** — the process by which people who have been denied power gain the ability to make choices about their own lives. **Naila Kabeer** describes it through three linked parts: resources, agency and achievements.
11. **Gender mainstreaming** — the strategy, adopted globally at the Beijing Conference in ==1995==, of checking every law, policy, budget and project for its effect on women and men before it is approved.
12. **Gender-based violence (GBV)** — violence directed at a person because of their gender, or which affects one gender disproportionately. The World Health Organization estimates that about ==1 in 3== women worldwide experiences physical or sexual violence in her lifetime.

::: fig grid | The twelve ideas that unlock the subject — each is taught in full in the topic shown.
@cols 4
1 · Patriarchy: the rule of men, treated as natural (all topics)
2 · Gender roles: tasks assigned by gender (T1)
3 · Socialisation: how the roles are learnt (T1)
4 · Social construction: made by society, so changeable (T1)
5 · Division of labour: paid men's work, unpaid women's work (T4, T5)
6 · Public and private: "the personal is political" (T2, T3)
7 · Masculinities and femininities: many forms, one dominant (T1, T7)
8 · Feminism: equality as belief and movement (T2, T3)
9 · Intersectionality: overlapping disadvantages (T2, T5)
10 · Empowerment: resources, agency, achievements (T4)
11 · Gender mainstreaming: check every policy (T3, T4)
12 · Gender-based violence: harm because of gender (T7)
:::

::: remember | The twelve ideas in one line each
Patriarchy (male rule) · gender roles (assigned tasks) · socialisation (learning them) · social construction (made, not born) · division of labour (paid and unpaid) · public/private (and the personal is political) · masculinities/femininities (many, one dominant) · feminism (equality as belief and movement) · intersectionality (overlapping disadvantages) · empowerment (gaining choice) · mainstreaming (checking every policy) · GBV (violence because of gender).
:::

## 0.4  The words you must know before anything else

The tables below give about a hundred terms, grouped by the topic in which each is taught. A star (★) marks a term you must be able to define in one sentence in the exam. Tick each term when you can explain it to a friend without looking.

### Topic 1 · The discipline and the social construction of gender

::: table
@widths 3.2,10.8
Term | Meaning
Gender Studies ★ | The multidisciplinary study of how gender is made, how it shapes power and life chances, and how it intersects with other identities.
Women's Studies ★ | The academic field, begun around 1969–70, that makes women the subject and the knowers of research, as part of the women's movement.
Multidisciplinary ★ | Drawing on many disciplines at once — sociology, psychology, economics, law, history, biology, literature.
Autonomy versus integration ★ | The debate over whether Women's/Gender Studies should be a separate department (autonomy) or be woven into every existing discipline (integration). Framed by Bowles and Klein (1983).
Androcentrism | Treating the male as the norm and the female as a deviation, as when medicine tested drugs only on men.
Social constructionism ★ | The theory that what seems natural (gender, even aspects of sex) is produced by social processes, language and history.
Essentialism | The opposite view: women and men have fixed, natural essences that explain their behaviour.
Biological determinism | The belief that biology alone decides behaviour and social roles.
Gender identity ★ | A person's inner sense of being a woman, a man, both, or neither.
Gender roles / norms ★ | The behaviours expected of each gender; norms are the unwritten rules that enforce them.
Socialisation ★ | Learning the norms of one's society, including gender norms, from family, school, media and religion.
Masculinity / femininity ★ | The sets of qualities a culture expects of men and of women.
Hegemonic masculinity ★ | Connell's term for the dominant, idealised form of masculinity that legitimates men's power over women and over "lesser" men.
Androgyny | Having both masculine and feminine traits; Sandra Bem (1974) argued androgynous people are the most adaptable.
Queer theory ★ | A theory from about 1990 (de Lauretis, Butler, Sedgwick) that questions fixed categories of sex, gender and sexuality and the norm of heterosexuality.
Performativity ★ | Butler's idea that gender is not something we are but something we repeatedly do; the repetition creates the illusion of a natural core.
Doing gender | West and Zimmerman's (1987) idea that gender is accomplished in everyday interaction.
Heteronormativity | The assumption that heterosexuality is the only normal sexuality and that gender comes in two opposite forms.
Intersex | Born with sex characteristics that do not fit typical definitions of male or female.
Transgender / khawaja sira | A person whose gender identity differs from the sex recorded at birth. *Khawaja sira* is the traditional South Asian term for gender-variant communities, recognised by Pakistan's Supreme Court in 2009.
Gendered language | Language that encodes gender bias, e.g. "chairman", "mankind", or Urdu idioms that praise men as brave and women as modest.
:::

### Topic 2 · Feminist theories

::: table
@widths 3.2,10.8
Term | Meaning
Feminism ★ | The belief in the social, political and economic equality of the sexes, and the movement to achieve it.
Liberal feminism ★ | Equality through equal rights, laws, education and opportunity within the existing system (Wollstonecraft, Mill, Friedan).
Radical feminism ★ | Patriarchy is the deepest oppression; it runs through family, sexuality and culture, so society must be transformed at the root (Millett, Firestone).
Marxist feminism ★ | Women's oppression comes from class society and private property; it will end with capitalism (Engels).
Socialist feminism ★ | Capitalism and patriarchy are two linked systems — "dual systems" — and both must be fought (Hartmann, Mitchell).
Psychoanalytic feminism ★ | Gender inequality is rooted in early childhood and the unconscious mind; it revises Freud (Horney, Chodorow, Mitchell).
Postmodern feminism ★ | There is no single "woman" or single truth; identities are made in language and discourse (Butler, Cixous, Irigaray, Kristeva).
Men's feminism / pro-feminism ★ | Men who support feminist goals and study how patriarchy shapes men (Connell, Kimmel; UN Women's HeForShe, 2014).
Black feminism | Feminism that centres the experience of Black women facing race, gender and class together (hooks, Collins, Crenshaw).
Postcolonial feminism | Critiques Western feminism for speaking for "Third World women" (Mohanty, Spivak).
Ecofeminism | Links the domination of women with the domination of nature (d'Eaubonne, 1974; Shiva).
Muslim / Islamic feminism | Seeks gender justice through re-reading Islamic sources (Amina Wadud, Fatima Mernissi, Riffat Hassan).
Misogyny | Hatred of, or contempt for, women.
Standpoint theory | Knowledge is shaped by social position; women's lived experience gives insight that dominant groups miss (Harding, Hartsock).
Écriture féminine | "Women's writing" — Hélène Cixous's call for writing from the female body and experience (1975).
Kyriarchy | Elisabeth Schüssler Fiorenza's term for interlocking systems of domination (gender, race, class, empire).
Matrix of domination | Patricia Hill Collins's (1990) term for how intersecting oppressions are organised across society.
Patriarchal bargain | Deniz Kandiyoti's (1988) term for the strategies women use to secure what they can within patriarchy.
:::

### Topic 3 · Feminist movements

::: table
@widths 3.2,10.8
Term | Meaning
Wave ★ | A period of intense feminist activity. First (c. 1848–1920: the vote), second (1960s–80s: equality in work, family, body), third (1990s–2000s: diversity), fourth (c. 2012–: digital activism, #MeToo).
Suffrage ★ | The right to vote. Suffragists campaigned lawfully; "suffragettes" (WSPU, from 1903) used militant tactics.
Seneca Falls Convention ★ | The 1848 meeting in New York, led by Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Lucretia Mott, that issued the Declaration of Sentiments.
CEDAW ★ | The UN Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women (adopted 1979; in force 1981) — "an international bill of rights for women". Pakistan acceded in 1996.
Beijing Platform for Action ★ | The plan adopted at the Fourth World Conference on Women, 1995: twelve critical areas of concern, and gender mainstreaming.
APWA ★ | All Pakistan Women's Association, founded in 1949 by Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan — welfare-based activism.
WAF ★ | Women's Action Forum, founded in 1981 to resist Zia-ul-Haq's discriminatory laws; the 12 February 1983 Lahore protest is its defining moment.
Aurat March ★ | Annual marches on International Women's Day (8 March), held in Pakistan since 2018.
NGO-isation ★ | The process by which a social movement turns into donor-funded, professionalised NGOs doing projects — often said to depoliticise it (Alvarez; Jad).
#MeToo | The movement against sexual harassment that went global in 2017 (the phrase was first used by Tarana Burke in 2006).
State feminism | Women's rights promoted from above by the state, e.g. Ayub Khan's Muslim Family Laws Ordinance of 1961.
:::

### Topic 4 · Gender and development

::: table
@widths 3.2,10.8
Term | Meaning
Development ★ | The process of improving people's lives — income, health, education, freedom. Gender analysis asks: development for whom?
Modernisation theory ★ | Poor countries develop by copying the path of the West — industry, cities, "modern" values (Rostow, 1960). Critics: women were left out.
Dependency theory ★ | Poor countries (the periphery) are kept poor by rich countries (the core) through unequal trade (Frank, Prebisch).
World-system theory ★ | Immanuel Wallerstein's (1974) view of one capitalist world economy of core, semi-periphery and periphery.
Structural functionalism ★ | Society is a system of parts that keep it stable; Parsons saw men's "instrumental" and women's "expressive" roles as functional.
WID ★ | Women in Development (1970s): add women to development projects; inspired by Ester Boserup (1970).
WAD ★ | Women and Development (late 1970s): women were always in development — but on unequal, exploitative terms set by global capitalism.
GAD ★ | Gender and Development (1980s): change the unequal power relations between women and men, not just women's condition.
Practical gender needs ★ | Immediate needs within existing roles — water, health care, income (Molyneux 1985; Moser 1989/1993).
Strategic gender interests ★ | Long-term needs that change women's position — legal rights, ending violence, equal voice.
Triple role | Moser's idea that women do reproductive, productive and community-managing work.
SAPs ★ | Structural Adjustment Programmes/Policies: IMF and World Bank loan conditions (from the 1980s) — cutting subsidies and spending — which fell hardest on women.
Globalisation ★ | The growing interconnection of economies and cultures across the world.
Feminisation of labour / poverty | More women drawn into low-paid, insecure work; more of the poor being women.
Global care chain | Arlie Hochschild's term for women migrating to care for richer families while others care for their own children.
Coloniality | The idea that colonial power relations continue after independence, including through "development" (Quijano, Lugones, Escobar).
Gender budgeting | Analysing a budget for its impact on women and men.
:::

### Topics 5 and 6 · Women in Pakistan; gender and governance

::: table
@widths 3.2,10.8
Term | Meaning
Maternal mortality ratio ★ | Deaths of women from pregnancy-related causes per 100,000 live births. Pakistan: ==186== (Pakistan Maternal Mortality Survey, 2019).
Female labour force participation ★ | The share of working-age women who are working or looking for work. Pakistan: ==24.4%== (Labour Force Survey 2024–25).
Gender parity index | The ratio of girls to boys in school; 1.0 means equality.
Glass ceiling ★ | The invisible barrier that stops women rising to the top of organisations.
Sticky floor / glass cliff | Women stuck in low-paid jobs / women promoted to leadership in a crisis, when failure is likely.
Informal sector | Unregistered work without contracts or protection — home-based workers, domestic workers, agricultural labour.
Governance ★ | The way power is exercised in managing a country's resources and affairs: participation, accountability, transparency, rule of law.
Quota ★ | A share of seats or posts reserved for women. Pakistan: ==60 of 336== National Assembly seats (since 2002; 60 of 342 before the 2018 merger of FATA changed the total).
Critical mass | The idea that women must form about ==30%== of a body before they can change its agenda (Dahlerup).
Descriptive vs substantive representation ★ | Women being present in parliament vs women's interests actually being advanced there.
Glass wall | Barriers that confine women to "soft" ministries like women's affairs and social welfare.
:::

### Topic 7 · Gender-based violence

::: table
@widths 3.2,10.8
Term | Meaning
Violence against women ★ | "Any act of gender-based violence that results in, or is likely to result in, physical, sexual or psychological harm or suffering to women" (UN Declaration, 1993).
Direct violence ★ | Visible violence with an identifiable perpetrator — beating, rape, acid attack, murder (Galtung).
Structural violence ★ | Harm built into social structures — poverty, discriminatory laws, lack of health care — with no single perpetrator (Galtung, 1969).
Cultural violence | Beliefs, customs and language that make direct and structural violence look normal (Galtung, 1990).
Honour killing ★ | The murder of a woman (or man) by relatives who believe she has brought "dishonour" on the family.
Domestic violence ★ | Abuse within the home — physical, sexual, psychological or economic.
Power and control wheel | The Duluth model (1981) showing how abusers use intimidation, isolation and economic control to dominate.
Cycle of violence | Lenore Walker's (1979) pattern: tension builds, incident, reconciliation, calm — and again.
Vani / swara | Giving girls in marriage to settle disputes between families; criminalised in Pakistan (2004 and 2011).
Jirga / panchayat | Informal councils of elders that sometimes order punishments against women, as in the Mukhtaran Mai case.
Technology-facilitated GBV | Online harassment, blackmail, image abuse and stalking; covered in Pakistan by PECA 2016.
:::

## 0.5  The thinkers you will meet

You do not need to memorise all of these now. But when a name appears in a Knowledge Base, come back to this table to see where the thinker fits. Quoting the right thinker in the right place is one of the cheapest ways to earn marks.

::: table | Forty names that carry the subject.
@widths 3.3,5.2,3.4,1.1
@font 9
Thinker | Main idea | Key work (year) | Topic
Mary Wollstonecraft | Women are not naturally inferior; they lack education | *A Vindication of the Rights of Woman* (1792) | T2
John Stuart Mill (with Harriet Taylor) | Legal subordination of women is wrong and harms society | *The Subjection of Women* (1869) | T2
Friedrich Engels | Women's oppression began with private property | *The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State* (1884) | T2
Simone de Beauvoir | Woman is made the "Other"; "one is not born … a woman" | *The Second Sex* (1949) | T1, T2
Betty Friedan | "The problem that has no name" — the unhappy housewife | *The Feminine Mystique* (1963) | T2, T3
Kate Millett | Patriarchy is political; "sexual politics" | *Sexual Politics* (1970) | T2
Shulamith Firestone | Biology and reproduction are the root of women's oppression | *The Dialectic of Sex* (1970) | T2
Ann Oakley | The sex/gender distinction in sociology; housework as work | *Sex, Gender and Society* (1972) | T1
Robert Stoller | First clear separation of sex and gender identity | *Sex and Gender* (1968) | T1
Karen Horney | Rejected Freud's "penis envy"; proposed "womb envy" | essays of the 1920s–30s | T2
Nancy Chodorow | Mothering reproduces gender in each generation | *The Reproduction of Mothering* (1978) | T2
Juliet Mitchell | Joined Marxism and psychoanalysis | *Psychoanalysis and Feminism* (1974) | T2
Heidi Hartmann | Capitalism and patriarchy as "dual systems" | "The Unhappy Marriage of Marxism and Feminism" (1979) | T2
Michel Foucault | Power produces knowledge about sexuality | *The History of Sexuality*, vol. 1 (1976) | T1
Judith Butler | Gender is performative; even sex is shaped by discourse | *Gender Trouble* (1990) | T1, T2
Eve Kosofsky Sedgwick | The binary of homo/heterosexual shapes modern culture | *Epistemology of the Closet* (1990) | T1
Teresa de Lauretis | Coined the term "queer theory" | essay (1991) | T1
Candace West and Don Zimmerman | Gender as something we "do" in interaction | "Doing Gender" (1987) | T1
Raewyn Connell | Hegemonic masculinity; gender as a social structure | *Gender and Power* (1987); *Masculinities* (1995) | T1, T7
Sandra Bem | Androgyny; gender schema theory | Bem Sex-Role Inventory (1974) | T1
Hélène Cixous | Écriture féminine — writing the female body | "The Laugh of the Medusa" (1975) | T2
Luce Irigaray; Julia Kristeva | French feminist psychoanalysis and language | 1970s–80s | T2
Elaine Showalter | Gynocriticism — studying women writers | *A Literature of Their Own* (1977) | T2
bell hooks | Feminism must include race and class; "feminism is for everybody" | *Ain't I a Woman?* (1981) | T2
Kimberlé Crenshaw | Coined "intersectionality" | 1989 law article | T2, T5
Patricia Hill Collins | The matrix of domination | *Black Feminist Thought* (1990) | T2
Chandra Talpade Mohanty | Western feminism homogenises "Third World women" | "Under Western Eyes" (1984/1986) | T2, T4
Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak | Can the subaltern speak? Strategic essentialism | 1988 essay | T2, T4
Deniz Kandiyoti | Bargaining with patriarchy | 1988 article | T2
Ester Boserup | Development ignored women's work in agriculture | *Woman's Role in Economic Development* (1970) | T4
Caroline Moser | Gender planning; practical and strategic needs; the triple role | *Gender Planning and Development* (1993) | T4
Maxine Molyneux | Practical versus strategic gender interests | 1985 article on Nicaragua | T4
Naila Kabeer | Empowerment as resources, agency, achievements | *Reversed Realities* (1994) | T4
Maria Mies | Patriarchy and capitalism on a world scale | *Patriarchy and Accumulation on a World Scale* (1986) | T4
Immanuel Wallerstein | World-system theory | *The Modern World-System* (1974) | T4
Vandana Shiva; Bina Agarwal | Ecofeminism; feminist environmentalism | *Staying Alive* (1988); 1992 article | T4
Johan Galtung | Direct, structural and cultural violence | 1969 and 1990 articles | T7
Lenore Walker | The cycle of violence; battered woman syndrome | *The Battered Woman* (1979) | T7
Khawar Mumtaz and Farida Shaheed | History of the women's movement in Pakistan | *Women of Pakistan: Two Steps Forward, One Step Back?* (1987) | T3
