:::

## 0.6  The whole syllabus in twenty minutes

Read these seven short stories once. They are the whole subject in outline. Every chapter you study later is a closer look at one part of one of these stories.

### Topic 1 · The discipline and the social construction of gender

In the late 1960s, women in American universities noticed that almost everything they were taught had been written by men, about men. They founded **Women's Studies** — the first courses at Cornell (==1969==) and the first programme at San Diego State (==1970==). By the 1980s the field widened into **Gender Studies**, which studies men and masculinities too, and the relations of power between genders. A long debate followed: should the field be its own department (**autonomy**) or be spread into every discipline (**integration**)? Gloria Bowles and Renate Duelli Klein set out the debate in ==1983==; most universities now do both. In Pakistan, the government created Women's Studies centres in five universities in ==1989==, and today many universities teach Gender Studies at degree level, though funding and social resistance remain problems.

At the heart of the field is the claim that gender is **socially constructed**. Sex is biological; gender is made by society through socialisation, language and institutions. Masculinity and femininity are not natural essences but patterns we learn and perform. The **nature versus culture** debate asks how much biology matters. **Queer theory** pushes the argument further: perhaps even the division into two sexes is partly a social decision. In Pakistan this debate reached the courts through the **Transgender Persons (Protection of Rights) Act 2018**, which the Federal Shariat Court partly struck down in ==2023== — an appeal was still pending in 2026.

### Topic 2 · Feminist theories and practice

Feminism agrees that women are disadvantaged, but its schools disagree on the cause. **Liberal** feminists blame unequal laws and opportunities and want reform. **Radical** feminists blame patriarchy itself and want transformation. **Marxist** feminists blame capitalism and private property; **socialist** feminists blame capitalism and patriarchy together. **Psychoanalytic** feminists look to childhood and the unconscious. **Postmodern** feminists deny that there is any single "woman" to liberate. **Men's feminism** asks what patriarchy does to men and how men can help end it. Newer currents — **intersectionality**, **Black**, **postcolonial**, **eco-** and **Muslim** feminism — have changed the field, and the examiner has asked about intersectionality twice (==2025==, ==2026==).

### Topic 3 · Feminist movements

In the West, the **first wave** (==1848–1920==) won the vote and legal personhood. The **second wave** (1960s–80s) fought for equality in work, education, the family and the body, under the slogan "the personal is political". The **third wave** (1990s–2000s) celebrated diversity and individuality; a **fourth wave** (from about 2012) uses social media — #MeToo is its emblem. At the **United Nations**, four world conferences on women (Mexico City ==1975==, Copenhagen ==1980==, Nairobi ==1985==, Beijing ==1995==) and **CEDAW** (==1979==) turned women's rights into global commitments. In **Pakistan**, the movement began with welfare and refugee work (APWA, ==1949==), won the Muslim Family Laws Ordinance (==1961==), turned confrontational against Zia-ul-Haq's Hudood laws (WAF, ==1981==), spread into NGOs in the 1990s, won a series of protective laws after ==2004==, and since ==2018== has taken to the streets and social media in the Aurat March.

### Topic 4 · Gender and development

Colonial rule and capitalism changed gender relations in the subcontinent — sometimes through reform (the ban on sati, ==1829==; the Child Marriage Restraint Act, ==1929==), often by hardening patriarchy and pushing women out of land and trade. After 1945, **modernisation theory** promised that poor countries would develop by copying the West; **dependency** and **world-system theory** answered that the West keeps them poor. None of these theories looked at women until **Ester Boserup** showed in ==1970== that development projects ignored women's farm work. This gave birth to **WID** (add women), then **WAD** (women were always in development, on unequal terms), then **GAD** (change power relations). The **structural adjustment** policies of the 1980s cut health and education budgets and fell hardest on women. **Globalisation** brought jobs in factories and call centres but also insecure, low-paid work, migration and new forms of patriarchal power. Today, **climate change** is the newest gender-and-development question — asked in ==2026== with reference to Pakistan's floods and heat waves.

### Topic 5 · Status of women in Pakistan

The Constitution of ==1973== promises equality (Article 25), women's full participation in national life (Article 34) and maternity protection (Article 37). Reality lags. Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== in the Global Gender Gap Report 2026 (after ranking last in 2025). Women's literacy is about ==53%== against about ==68%== for men (Census 2023). Only about a quarter of working-age women are in the labour force (==24.4%==, LFS 2024–25). Maternal mortality, anaemia and malnutrition remain high. A strong body of pro-women laws has been passed since 2004, but enforcement is weak. This topic studies health, education, employment and law, and the data behind each — and it is where the newest questions (the gender gap, the digital divide, intersectional disadvantage) now come from.

### Topic 6 · Gender and governance

Good governance needs women's participation — as **voters**, **candidates** and **representatives**. Women won the vote first in New Zealand (==1893==) after the **suffragist** movement. Pakistan's women voted from the start, but they face missing identity cards, family control of their votes and agreements between candidates to bar women from polling. Candidates face money, violence, party gatekeeping and family resistance. Representatives face tokenism and exclusion from real power. **Quotas** — ==60== reserved National Assembly seats since ==2002==, ==33%== in local government under the ==2000== Devolution Plan, and at least ==5%== of party tickets under the Elections Act ==2017== — have raised numbers. Whether they have raised women's influence is the question the examiner keeps asking (==2016==, ==2021==, ==2024==, ==2025==).

### Topic 7 · Gender-based violence and the case studies

Violence against women ranges from **direct** acts (beating, rape, acid attacks, honour killing) to **structural** harm built into laws, poverty and institutions. Theories explain it through patriarchy and power (feminist theory), learning (social learning theory), the family's stresses (family violence theories) and layers of causes (the ecological model). Pakistan has passed many laws — against harassment (==2010==), acid crime (==2011==), anti-women customs (==2011==), honour killing loopholes (==2016==), domestic violence (the provinces from 2013; the Punjab Act of ==2016==; Islamabad in ==2025==) and rape (==2021==). The three **case studies** show the problem and the resistance: **Mukhtaran Mai**, gang-raped on a village council's order in ==2002==, who turned her compensation into schools; **Malala Yousafzai**, shot by the Taliban in ==2012== for demanding girls' education, who became the youngest Nobel laureate in ==2014==; and **Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy**, whose two Oscar-winning documentaries — on acid attacks (==2012==) and honour killing (==2016==) — pushed the state to act.

## 0.7  The timeline behind the paper

::: fig timeline | The timeline behind the paper: the dates that recur across all seven topics.
@layout vertical
== Roots of thought
1792: Mary Wollstonecraft, A Vindication of the Rights of Woman
1829: Sati banned in British India (Bengal Sati Regulation)
1848: Seneca Falls Convention opens the first wave
1869: J. S. Mill, The Subjection of Women
1884: Engels, The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State
== The first wave and the vote
1893: New Zealand — first country to give women the national vote
1918: British women over 30 win the vote (equal franchise in 1928)
1920: The 19th Amendment gives American women the vote
1929: Child Marriage Restraint Act in British India
1949: Simone de Beauvoir, The Second Sex; APWA founded in Pakistan
== The second wave and the UN
1961: Muslim Family Laws Ordinance in Pakistan
1963: Betty Friedan, The Feminine Mystique
1970: Boserup's Woman's Role in Economic Development; Millett; Firestone
1972: Ann Oakley, Sex, Gender and Society
1975: International Women's Year; First World Conference on Women, Mexico City
1979: CEDAW adopted; Hudood Ordinances in Pakistan
1981: Women's Action Forum founded
1983: 12 February protest in Lahore; Bowles and Klein on autonomy vs integration
1985: Nairobi Conference; DAWN network launches the GAD critique
1988: Benazir Bhutto becomes the first woman prime minister of a Muslim-majority country
== Gender, globalisation and rights
1989: Crenshaw coins "intersectionality"; Women's Studies centres set up in five Pakistani universities
1990: Butler, Gender Trouble
1995: Fourth World Conference on Women, Beijing — gender mainstreaming
1996: Pakistan accedes to CEDAW
2000: NCSW set up; 33% seats for women in local government; UNSCR 1325
2002: 60 reserved seats for women in the National Assembly; Mukhtaran Mai case
2006: Protection of Women (Criminal Laws Amendment) Act
2010: UN Women created; workplace harassment law in Pakistan
== The present
2012: Malala shot; Saving Face wins an Oscar
2014: Malala wins the Nobel Peace Prize
2015: SDG 5 — gender equality by 2030
2016: Anti-honour-killing law; Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act; second Oscar for Obaid-Chinoy
2017: #MeToo goes global; Elections Act (5% tickets to women)
2018: First Aurat March; Transgender Persons Act
2021: Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act
2022: Floods submerge a third of Pakistan
2023: Federal Shariat Court strikes down parts of the Transgender Act
2025: Beijing+30; Islamabad child-marriage law (minimum age 18); Pakistan last in the Gender Gap Report
2026: Pakistan rises to 143rd of 145 in the Gender Gap Report
:::

## 0.8  Six habits of mind for a Gender Studies paper

1. **Define before you discuss.** Every good answer opens by defining its key terms — gender, feminism, patriarchy, empowerment — in one clean sentence each. Half the candidates never do, and the examiner notices.
2. **Name the theory.** Do not write "some people think…". Write "Liberal feminists, from Wollstonecraft to Friedan, argue…". A named theory with a named thinker is worth three vague sentences.
3. **Always ask "why?"** Gender Studies is about causes. If you describe a problem — low literacy, honour killing, few women in parliament — go on to explain *why* it exists, using a theory.
4. **Bring it home to Pakistan.** Every theory has a Pakistani example; every Pakistani fact illustrates a theory. The answer that moves between the two is the answer that scores.
5. **Use numbers carefully.** One correct, dated statistic from a named source beats five doubtful ones. "Female labour force participation was 24.4% (Labour Force Survey 2024–25)" is evidence; "only 10% of women work" is a guess.
6. **Be fair, then be clear.** Present the strongest version of the view you disagree with, then give your own reasoned position. That is what a civil servant is paid to do.

## 0.9  How to handle the sensitive debates

Gender Studies touches questions on which Pakistani society is divided. The examiner is not testing whether you are "for" or "against" feminism. He or she is testing whether you can think clearly and write responsibly about a contested issue. Use this approach on every sensitive question:

::: balance | The four-step approach
1. State the issue neutrally, with its facts (what the law says, what happened, what the data shows).
2. Give the main positions fairly — including the strongest argument of each side.
3. Anchor your judgement in the **Constitution of Pakistan** (equality under Article 25; protection of the family under Article 35; participation of women under Article 34; Islamic provisions under Articles 2A, 31 and 227), in Pakistan's **international commitments** (CEDAW, the SDGs), and in **evidence**.
4. Conclude with a practical, dignified way forward that a government could actually adopt.
-> This is the voice of a civil servant, not an activist or a preacher — and it is the voice that scores.
:::

::: table | The sensitive debates you will meet, and where.
@widths 3.4,7.6,2
Debate | Why it is sensitive | Topic
Religion and women's rights | Some see Islam as the source of women's rights; others see certain interpretations as obstacles. Muslim feminists argue for re-reading the sources. | T2, T3, T5
The transgender law of 2018 | Supporters see a human-rights law; critics object to self-perceived gender identity. The Federal Shariat Court ruled in 2023; an appeal was pending in 2026. | T1, T5
The Aurat March | Supporters see a democratic protest; critics object to some slogans as offensive to local values. | T3
"Western" versus local feminism | Critics call feminism a foreign import; historians show a long local tradition of women's activism. | T2, T3
NGO-isation and foreign funding | Donor money built capacity but, critics argue, depoliticised the movement. | T3, T4
Quotas | Supporters see a fast track to equality; critics see tokenism and dynastic politics. | T6
:::

::: trap | Never do these
- Never mock a religious view or a religious scholar. Never mock feminists either.
- Never generalise about "all men" or "all women", or about any province, ethnicity or sect.
- Never quote a Quranic verse or hadith you are not sure of. If you use the Islamic perspective, keep it accurate, brief and respectful.
- Never present an allegation as a fact. Say "critics argue…" or "the court held…".
:::
